A||||8||ponya||Brake bleeding||||5||
Z||000000||ponya||11-04-2000||08:10 PM||||Just got done doing the rear brake upgrade and installing a new "E" brake cable. We now begarn to bleed the brakes. We have been bleeding the brakes for two hours now. They have finally begun to grab hold. My question is, is it normal to have to bleed the brakes over and over and over to get them to function properly. We have decided to call it quits for tonight, since we think we have made some progress, and start again tomorrow morning. Never had to do this before, with any car I've owned. Normal???? An imput appreciated.  Thanks  Bob (86SE)||||reg||5
Z||000001||polomeno||11-04-2000||08:33 PM||||It shoudlnt take that long.. are you bleeding them in the right order??  passenger rear / driver rear /passanger front / driver front/  if you dont do it this way you wont get all air out ..<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-04-2000||09:09 PM||||How far apart did you have the system? If you had the hoses off the calipers you need to replace the coppers.<P>get under the dash and check the master cyl. it could be leaking or sucking air.<P>try tapping the calipers with a rubber/plastic hammer. you could have an air bubble clinging to the side someplace.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||MoneypitGT||11-04-2000||09:15 PM||||I am having a similiar problem with bleeding the brakes. I replaced both rear calipers on my 87 GT and now have no pedal. We bled them for ever, still nothing. I thought the master cylinder went, so I replaced it with a rebuilt. I bench bled the mc and bled all the lines in the right order 3 times, but I still have tiny bubbles in some of the fluid, and partial pedal. If I let it sit, the pedal goes to the floor. If I pump the pedal it seems to be almost normal. Any ideas?<P>    Chris||||reg||9
Z||000004||Oreif||11-04-2000||09:22 PM||||When I replaced my calipers, I filled the resevoir with brake fluid (left cover off), opened the passenger side rear bleeder screw and waited about 15 minutes. When I saw fluid oozing out I closed the bleeder and opened the driverside rear. I did this in the order listed above. Once all the lines and calipers were "gravity bled" We went back and did the "pump and hold" bleeding and it took about 5 cycles to bleed each line. The gravity bleeding removes about 80-90% of the air in the system and doesn't create a lot of bubbles. It is so much easier than trying to pump an empty system. <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-04-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000005||Rodrv6||11-05-2000||12:02 AM||||I've had great success with suction bleeding at the calipers.  When I reassembled my car the brake system was totally dry-lines, master cylinder, everything.  I used a bleeder that sucks the fluid out at the bleed fittings and was able to get a good firm pedal with no trouble.  The other advantage is that 1 person can do the whole thing.  You do have to keep an eye on the level in the reservoir so you don't pull air in.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting :)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||11-05-2000||07:33 AM||||Another option for the 1 man job is SpeedBleeders.  They look like stock bleeder valves, but have a check valve installed in them.  Loosen the bleeder, attach a clear tube, and pump the brakes until you don't see any more bubbles, then tighten the bleeder.  If I can do it, anyone can.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tom88gt||11-05-2000||10:44 AM||||Speedbleeders rule!  Put mine on last night.  Oh so easy to bleed.  I ordered one for the clutch too but they sent the wrong one :-( so I'm still doing that the old fashioned way.||||reg||14
Z||000008||Bob Englert||11-05-2000||08:56 PM||||Where can I get speed bleeders for my cars?<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
